{"kind":"report","text":"changes-requested on SKILL.md at exact head 691b9ba334f2120cb6cded8f9ff501b8a39c5e00, for one specific and cheap defect. The condensation is good work and I want it to land; this is a one-clause repair, not a rethink.\n\nWHAT I DID. A rule-by-rule comparison of 691b9ba3^ against 691b9ba3, not an impression of the diff. I enumerated the normative content of the predecessor and accounted for every item in the successor as surviving in place, surviving by pointer, or weakened. For each of the six cuts you justified by pointer I opened the target and confirmed it carries the removed text. I re-ran both gates myself at the exact tree with -count=1.\n\nTHE FINDING: the `say` bullet now states something the implementation does not do.\n\nPredecessor: \"A unique effective-roster `@name` mention keeps the conversation for every currently live session of that actor and places it in the priority inbox of each session that registered inbox support.\"\n\nSuccessor (SKILL.md:69-71): \"A unique effective-roster `@name` mention reaches the priority inbox of every live session of that actor.\"\n\nThe predecessor carried two facts with two different scopes: conversation retention covers every currently live session, while priority-inbox placement covers only sessions that registered inbox support. The successor applies the wider scope to the narrower effect, and the result is false. docs/reference/mcp/say.md:41-43 says so directly: \"A session receives a pending priority-inbox reference only when it registered the current inbox protocol. Browser and older-adapter sessions are not enqueued.\" docs/reference/architecture.md:171 and docs/concepts/components.md:77 agree.\n\nThis matters more here than it would elsewhere because of the frontmatter: SKILL.md is \"Normative for agent actors; the implementation must match this contract.\" As written the implementation does not match, and the mismatch is the kind an actor would act on — someone reading only SKILL.md would expect a mention to reach every live session's inbox and could read a correctly-not-enqueued browser session as a delivery bug.\n\nIt also loses the retention fact entirely: that a mention keeps the conversation alive for every live session, which is what stops a conversation evaporating when the sender leaves. That is a real property with no remaining mention in SKILL.md.\n\nSUGGESTED REPAIR, one clause, your wording to choose: \"A unique effective-roster `@name` mention keeps the conversation for every live session of that actor and reaches the priority inbox of each session that registered inbox support.\" That restores both scopes in one sentence and is still shorter than the predecessor.\n\nEVERYTHING ELSE SURVIVES. I checked each pointered cut by opening the target:\n- per-tool mechanics to docs/reference/mcp/ — presence.md:67 carries per-session activity reporting; state.md:57 carries body.to fingerprint signing and the live-roster requirement; wait.md:79-86 carries reset replay, the `degraded` cursor and `available: false`; work.md:61-63 carries the cursor/head binding, and states it more strongly than the predecessor did (\"a moved head is an explicit refusal: restart the query\").\n- dated superseded-world rules to docs/reference/gs/merge.md — all three cases are there: judged as of the verdict (line 75), already-superseded-at-signing refuses (106-107), moved-after-verdict is recorded not refused (107), undated refuses (99), with `world_superseded_at` named at 85.\n- the priority-chat-repeats rule stated once — survives in the Tools preamble (\"Frames repeat until you `ack` them, because reading is not acknowledging\"), with the exact-thread-handle detail in the `ack` bullet.\n- artifact-rests-on-promise consolidated into discipline 8 — correct, and the no-promise alternative came with it (\"or on the request when you made no promise\"); the work loop cross-references discipline 8.\n- intake pointing at discipline 11 — the re-confirm triad (conditions, addressee's availability, governing decision) is intact in discipline 11, and \"recorded, not refused\" survives in both discipline 8 and 11.\n- the two sequence-number bullets merged — both rules survive, including that #N means nothing outside its workroom.\n\nLosses I judged acceptable, listed so you can disagree: several rationale clauses are gone while their rules remain — \"that supersession is what makes the prose flare, and it is yours to make because you are the one who moved the world\"; \"so the area settles on one granularity\"; \"not warnings to click through\"; \"a reader at the live artifact can never get from there to the proposal\". Cutting motivation while keeping the obligation is what a concision pass is for.\n\nOne non-blocking observation. Discipline 8 now names *unable to flare* and *succession not recorded* without saying what triggers each, where the predecessor said which condition produces which. The triggers are documented more fully than SKILL.md ever had them (docs/concepts/staleness.md:106-113, docs/reference/gs/status.md:179), so nothing is lost from the corpus, but a reader of SKILL.md alone can no longer tell the two apart. A short pointer there would close it. Not a condition of satisfaction; do not respin for this alone.\n\nTwo things I checked that are NOT findings. The bare-directory link at SKILL.md:29 follows established repo convention — docs/README.md:58, docs/how-to/configure-an-agent.md:163 and docs/concepts/components.md:64 and :113 all link bare reference directories, including `../reference/mcp/` — and every page in that directory is individually reachable from docs/README.md:83-94. And the head is 7 commits behind main (5ff5d4db) but main has not touched SKILL.md since the merge-base 23f691d6, so there is no content conflict; that is ordinary staleness, recorded rather than refused.\n\nARCHITECTURE. This is documentation, not code: no package boundary or interface moves, and no layer in docs/reference/architecture.md changes contract, so that page needs no update in this head. The relevant architectural question for this file is whether it still binds the implementation it governs, and with the `say` clause repaired it does. The relocation of mechanics into docs/reference/mcp/ strengthens rather than weakens that binding, because each tool's contract now has one authoritative page instead of two partial statements that could drift.\n\nSECURITY. No constraint doing real work was removed. Attribution (\"Acts are signed as you. Never speak for another actor\"), the secrets rule (\"Ephemeral is not secret. Never put secrets in either channel\"), and the authority rules all survive verbatim or with meaning intact: ratify still confers force only where the active kind definition names you as satisfier, with authority from the live roster and not from being human or agent; bare supersede is still admitted only from the target's author or a ratifier; declining is still two actors, never the addressee alone. The silent-citation warning survives intact, which matters because it is the one failure mode that reports success while connecting to nothing. The `say` defect is an accuracy problem, not a security one: it overstates delivery, and no authority or trust boundary moves.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The cut is already the simplification and I am not asking for more. 383 lines to 336 and 23382 to 20515 bytes, with the reductions falling on repetition and relocated mechanics rather than on rules. I found no remaining duplication worth a second pass, and I would resist cutting further: the prose that remains is carrying obligations.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact tree rather than cited from your report:\n- go test -count=1 . (root public-surface package, including TestLocalMarkdownLinks): ok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq 0.249s\n- go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/...: ok github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/docset 74.030s\nBoth green. I used -count=1 deliberately so a cached pass predating the edit could not stand in for one after it. The worktree at this head is clean and the head is pushed to origin/request/skill-md-concision.\n\nRepair the `say` clause and re-request; I will re-review promptly and expect to approve.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6d0f177e6a5b86c126c9e0195b21461c4ff05d3c","head":"691b9ba334f2120cb6cded8f9ff501b8a39c5e00","verdict":"changes-requested"}}